# Copy to .env to override. The demo needs no secret; every value below is a default.

# Image tag used for the services built from this repository.
PLEIADES_VERSION=dev
# Version compiled into the .NET services. It must be a version number, for example 0.0.1 or 0.0.1-mybranch.
PLEIADES_BUILD_VERSION=0.0.1

# Third-party images.
MOSQUITTO_VERSION=2
# MongoDB 8.0 does not start on some very recent Linux kernels; pick a newer tag there.
MONGODB_VERSION=8.0
# The LiteLLM gateway of one's own, with the profile "gateway" only (see below).
LITELLM_VERSION=v1.109.1

# Published ports on the host.
MQTT_PORT=1883
MQTT_WS_PORT=9001
# The browser hears live values at the broker, MQTT over WebSocket: at the host of the page and MQTT_WS_PORT.
# Behind a proxy, name the whole address instead, for example wss://demo.example.com/mqtt.
MQTT_WS_URL=
TIMESERIES_API_PORT=5500
EVENTS_API_PORT=5510
SITES_API_PORT=5540
GENUI_MCP_PORT=5520
APP_PORT=5600
# The web pages of the cutting simulators. They answer on this computer only.
CUTTING_LASER_2D_PORT=5611
CUTTING_TUBE_PORT=5612
CUTTING_PUNCH_PORT=5613
# The address of the host the example app answers on: this computer only. Its chat has no sign-in, so whoever
# reaches the port can chat with the language model below, at the cost of the owner of its key, and read every
# chat. 0.0.0.0 opens the port to the network; do that only in a network you trust.
APP_BIND=127.0.0.1
# The address of the host the Sites API answers on: this computer only. Writing to it needs no sign-in in the
# demo, so whoever reaches the port can add and remove sites and machines. 0.0.0.0 opens it to the network.
SITES_BIND=127.0.0.1

# OPC UA: the adapter and a simulated machine. See source/Pleiades.Playground.OpcUa.Adapter/README.md.
# The management API of the adapter. It has no sign-in: whoever reaches the port can add and remove
# machines. 0.0.0.0 opens it to the network; do that only in a network you trust.
OPCUA_ADAPTER_PORT=5560
OPCUA_ADAPTER_BIND=127.0.0.1
# The image of the TRUMPF OPC UA simulator, and the port of its web UI. umati publishes the image; name
# another one only to try a simulator of your own build.
OPC_SIMULATOR_IMAGE=ghcr.io/umati/trumpf/edge/opc-simulator:1.4.0-alpha3
OPC_SIMULATOR_UI_PORT=8090
# Where the chat of the example app finds the adapter: its tools, and its API for the buttons that add and
# remove a machine. Unset, they point to the adapter of the stack; empty, the chat has no OPC UA.
#OPCUA_MCP_URL=http://opcua-adapter:8080/mcp
#OPCUA_ADAPTER_URL=http://opcua-adapter:8080

# How long the services keep data (d.hh:mm:ss).
DATA_RETENTION=30.00:00:00

# The origins a card file of the generative UI backend may load from, separated by commas. A card loads
# nothing of its own. It takes the font of the host that shows it, and a host that serves its font from an
# origin of its own must find that origin here: https://assets.claude.ai for the Claude apps, for example.
# Empty: none, and the card falls back to a system font there.
CARD_RESOURCE_DOMAINS=

# The language models of the chat in the example app. Without them the chat says so; everything else works.
# They come from a LiteLLM gateway, which reaches Anthropic, OpenAI and models of one's own behind one address
# and one key. At the hackathon, the gateway of the hub: its address and key are in the handbook.
# The chat offers the models the gateway marks for chat under GET <LLM_BASE_URL>/models, in its order, and the
# first one answers until a chat picks another. Dictation takes the first model it marks for speech to text.
# The key stays on the server. But everyone who reaches the app can use it through the chat: see APP_BIND.
# More: apps/app/README.md, "Language models".
# LLM_BASE_URL: the address up to /v1, as the app reaches it from its container, where `localhost` is the
# container. A gateway on this computer is http://host.docker.internal:<port>/v1.
# LLM_API_KEY: the key the gateway gave you.
LLM_BASE_URL=
LLM_API_KEY=

# Without the gateway of the hackathon, with a key of Anthropic or of OpenAI: the stack has a LiteLLM gateway
# of its own. Set LLM_BASE_URL=http://litellm:4000/v1, and LLM_API_KEY to a key of your own choosing that
# begins with sk-, which becomes the master key of that gateway; put the key of the provider below, and start
# with docker compose --profile gateway up -d. Its models are in stacks/litellm/config.yaml.
# Without the profile the two lines are not read and nothing is downloaded.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Optional: the adapter of a SimpleCAM machine (Compose profile: simplecam). It needs a SimpleCAM backend, so it
# starts only on request: docker compose --profile simplecam up -d, or name it in COMPOSE_PROFILES below:
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=simplecam. It is not part of the Margo app.
# SIMPLECAM_SSE_URL: the SSE stream of the backend as the container reaches it. A backend on this computer is
# host.docker.internal; on another computer, name its address, here in the private .env and not in a commit.
SIMPLECAM_SSE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:5450/stream/machine-9042/state

# Optional Ultimaker 3 slicer and MCP service (Compose profile: ultimaker).
# Set COMPOSE_PROFILES=ultimaker on the deployment host to start both services from published images.
# Profiles combine with a comma: COMPOSE_PROFILES=ultimaker,gateway also starts the own LiteLLM gateway.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=
# The chat of the example app gets the tools of an optional service when its MCP server is named here, as
# name=address, separated by commas. The name is the prefix of its tools, the address is the one the app
# reaches from its container: APP_MCP_SERVERS=ultimaker=http://ultimaker-api:8080/mcp
# Name only services whose profile is in COMPOSE_PROFILES. The chat has no sign-in: everyone who reaches the
# app can then use every tool of such a server, also one that starts or orders something. A chat can switch
# such a server off under "Tools" in its message field. A new chat begins as the last one in the same page
# was left, and with all of them after the page is loaded again. Empty: none.
# Documentation: docs=http://host.docker.internal:3202/mcp from Docker Desktop or Docker Engine on Linux
# (app maps host.docker.internal to host-gateway); the MCP listener and published port must be reachable,
# or docs=http://127.0.0.1:3202/mcp when the Playground runs directly on this computer.
APP_MCP_SERVERS=
# The example app with or without its showcases: pages made for one demo or one fair, which may show made-up
# data. Empty: the app alone, the image pleiades.playground.app, with nothing of a showcase in it. Set to
# anything, for example APP_SHOWCASES=true: the image pleiades.playground.app.showcases, the same app built
# with all of them. It is read when the image is pulled or built, not while the app runs: after a change,
# docker compose up -d app takes the other image. "false" is not empty: to switch them off, leave it empty.
APP_SHOWCASES=
# The page "OSACA cell" of the example app shows the OSACA cell in 3D from its manufacturer values, the topics
# <root>/oem/... . The broker it listens to, as the app reaches it from its container. It only subscribes.
OSACA_MQTT_URL=mqtt://broker:1883
# The address of the OSACA simulation, as the app reaches it from its container, e.g. http://<host>:<port>. The
# app loads the 3D model of the cell from there and starts its job there (POST /api/cnc/cmd). Empty: the page
# shows the values, but no model, and its button says that the address is missing.
OSACA_SIM_URL=
# Replace the example URL with the printer address reachable from that host's Docker network.
ULTIMAKER_PRINTER_URL=http://ultimaker.local/
ULTIMAKER_BIND=127.0.0.1
ULTIMAKER_API_PORT=5530
ULTIMAKER_ENABLE_PRINTING=false
ULTIMAKER_SLICE_TIMEOUT_SECOND=180
ULTIMAKER_AUTH_ENABLED=false
ULTIMAKER_AUTH_METADATA_ADDRESS=
ULTIMAKER_AUTH_VALID_ISSUER=
ULTIMAKER_AUTH_AUDIENCE=
ULTIMAKER_SERIAL_NUMBER=UM3-01
ULTIMAKER_POLL_INTERVAL_SECOND=2
ULTIMAKER_MQTT_URL=mqtt://broker:1883
ULTIMAKER_MQTT_USER=
ULTIMAKER_MQTT_PASSWORD=

# Optional: a juice bar as a tool (Compose profile: juicebar). It needs a machine that runs MixerControl.
# Start it with docker compose --profile juicebar up -d, or name it in COMPOSE_PROFILES above on the
# deployment host: COMPOSE_PROFILES=ultimaker,juicebar starts both.
# JUICEBAR_URL: the address of the machine as the container reaches it. Replace the example.
# JUICEBAR_ENABLE_ORDERING: true places orders at the real machine; a person still presses its start button.
# false makes the service read-only. JUICEBAR_MAX_QUEUE_LENGTH: no order while this many wait.
# JUICEBAR_BIND: 127.0.0.1 keeps REST and MCP on this computer; the service has no sign-in.
# JUICEBAR_SERIAL_NUMBER: the serial number of the machine, the last part of its machine id in the MQTT topics,
# <manufacturer>_<model>_<serial number>: trumpf_JuiceBar_JUICEBAR-01 by default. JUICEBAR_MANUFACTURER_URI, the
# web address of the manufacturer, gives the first part (its domain without www. and its ending), JUICEBAR_MODEL
# the second; JUICEBAR_MANUFACTURER_NAME is the name a display shows. JUICEBAR_LANGUAGE: en or de, for the names.
# For the chat of the example app to read the card and order a drink, add the service to APP_MCP_SERVERS above:
# juicebar=http://juicebar-api:8080/mcp
JUICEBAR_URL=http://juicebar.local:3000/
JUICEBAR_ENABLE_ORDERING=true
JUICEBAR_MAX_QUEUE_LENGTH=3
JUICEBAR_BIND=127.0.0.1
JUICEBAR_API_PORT=5550
JUICEBAR_SERIAL_NUMBER=JUICEBAR-01
JUICEBAR_MANUFACTURER_URI=https://www.trumpf.com
JUICEBAR_MANUFACTURER_NAME=TRUMPF
JUICEBAR_MODEL=JuiceBar
JUICEBAR_LANGUAGE=en
# Sign-in for REST and MCP, as in the other APIs. true needs the three values below; reading then needs the
# permission juicebar:read and an order juicebar:write. Switch it on before JUICEBAR_BIND opens the port.
JUICEBAR_AUTH_ENABLED=false
JUICEBAR_AUTH_METADATA_ADDRESS=
JUICEBAR_AUTH_VALID_ISSUER=
JUICEBAR_AUTH_AUDIENCE=
